\v 19 \add But\add* then¦157942 in the¦157943 night¦157947, \add *her¦157948\add* son¦157943 died¦157942 because she¦157949 \add ≈rolled over onto\add* him¦157950.
\v 20 \add ≈So¦157952\add* she¦157952 \add ≈got¦157955 up\add* in¦157963 the middle¦157953 of¦157958 the night¦157954 and¦157955 took¦157955 my son from¦157959 beside¦157959 me¦157959, as your¦157960 female servant¦157960 was¦157961 asleep¦157961. She¦157962 \add ≈left her¦157963 dead¦157967 son with¦157963 me, and¦157964 took my son¦157966 to her¦157966 bed\add*.
\v 21 \add ≈When¦157971\add* I woke up in¦157972 the morning¦157972 \add ready\add* to¦157973 breastfeed¦157973 my son¦157976, \add ≈to my¦157976 horror\add* he¦157979 was¦157986 dead. \add However\add* when I examined him¦157981 closely¦157980 in¦157982 the light, more shock, it¦157986 wasn't \add actually\add* my son!”
\p
\v 22 “No¦157994, my¦158006 son is the living¦157997 one,” the other¦157993 woman¦157992 said¦158001, “and¦157998 your¦157998 son is the¦157999 dead¦157999 one.”
\p “No, \em your\em* son is the dead one,” the first¦158000 woman \add ≈countered\add*, “and \em my\em* son is the living one!”
\p Then¦157991 they¦158008 \add continued to\add* \add ≈argue¦158008 in¦158009 front¦158009 of¦158009\add* the¦157997 king¦158010.
\p
\v 23 The¦158013 king¦158013 spoke¦158012, “\add ≈So¦158012 both of¦158018 you are claiming that your¦158020 baby is¦158018 the¦158019 one that's alive¦158019 and¦158012 that the¦158021 dead¦158021 one belongs to the other¦158022\add*.”
\v 24 Then \add *he¦158032\add* \add ≈ordered¦158032\add*, “Get¦158034 me¦158035 a sword¦158037.” \add ≈So\add* they¦158038 brought¦158038 a sword¦158039 to¦158040 the¦158040 king¦158041.
\v 25 Then¦158043 the¦158044 king¦158044 \add ≈commanded\add*, “\add ≈Cut¦158045\add* the living¦158049 child¦158048 into¦158050 two¦158050 pieces and¦158043 give¦158051 \add ≈them half¦158054 each\add*.”
\p
\v 26 \add ≈But¦158061\add* the \add ≈mother of¦158065 the surviving baby\add* felt compassion¦158073 for¦158083 her son¦158076 and¦158061 \add ≈yelled\add*, “Please, my¦158080 master¦158080, give¦158081 the baby to¦158083 her¦158083—you certainly¦158088 mustn't \add ≈kill¦158088\add* him¦158091!”
\p \add ≈Meanwhile the¦158062 other¦158092 woman¦158062\add* was¦158093 saying¦158093, “\add ≈Well then¦158061, neither¦158094 of us will¦158101 have¦158072 him. Cut¦158102 him in half\add*!”
\p
\v 27 The¦158105 king¦158105 \add ≈spoke¦158106 up\add*, “Don't¦158115 harm the baby. Give¦158107 the living¦158113 child¦158112 to¦158109 the \add first\add* woman—she's¦158117 his¦158118 mother¦158118.”
\p
\v 28 All¦158121 Yisrael¦158123 \add eventually\add* heard¦158120 \add ≈about¦158121 this case\add* that¦158127 the¦158129 king¦158129 had judged¦158126, and¦158120 they \add ≈felt an awesome respect for¦158133\add* \add *him\add* because¦158133 they¦158134 saw¦158134 that he¦158128 had¦158128 God's¦158138 wisdom¦158137 to do¦158140 justice¦158141 within¦158139 him¦158139.
\c 4
