\p \add ≈There was also¦158398 one¦158402 district governor¦158401 over Yehudah\add*.
\s1 Shelomoh's prosperity
\rem /s1 Magnificence of Solomon's Rule; Solomon's Daily Provisions; Solomon's Prosperity and Wisdom; Solomon's Prosperous Reign
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 20 \add Now\add* Yehudah and¦158407 Yisrael¦158407 had become as numerous¦158408 as¦158409 the sand¦158409 on the¦158414 seashore. They¦158416 \add had plenty\add* to¦158415 eat¦158416 and¦158417 drink¦158417 and they¦158416 were¦158418 contented.
\v 21 Shelomoh \add ≈controlled\add* all the kingdoms from the \add Euphrates\add* River to the land of the Philistines and as far as the Egyptian border, \add so they\add* brought tribute to \add *him\add* and \add ≈followed his orders\add* \add ≈during his lifetime\add*.\x + \xo 4:21: \xt Gen 15:18; 2Ch 9:26.\x*
\p
\v 22 The \add ≈daily requirements for the palace included\add* five tonnes of fine flour and eleven tonnes of coarse flour,
\v 23 ten fattened cattle, twenty pasture cattle, one hundred sheep, \add wild game including\add* deer, gazelle, and roebuck, and fattened fowls.
\p
\v 24 He \add ≈controlled all the kingdoms west of the Euphrates\add* from Tifsah \add in the north-east\add* to Gaza \add in the south-west\add*, and there was peace with all \add ≈his neighbours\add*.
\v 25 \add ≈So\add* all Yehudah and Yisrael lived peacefully, from Dan \add in the north\add* to Be'er-Sheva \add in the south\add*—\add ≈everyone focusing on their own property\add*.
\p
\v 26 Shelomoh had forty thousand stalls for his chariot horses, and twelve thousand horsemen.\x + \xo 4:26: \xt 1Ki 10:26; 2Ch 1:14; 9:25.\x*
\v 27 \add ≈The district governors provided supplies for the palace—one governor for each\add* month. They ensured that nothing was lacking for the king and \add ≈his visitors\add*.
\v 28 They also provided the barley and straw for the \add ≈chariot horses and the other horses\add*, \add ≈delivered to the designated locations\add*.
\s1 Shelomoh's wisdom
\rem /s1 Solomon's Wisdom; Fame of Solomon's Wisdom
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 29 God gave Shelomoh wisdom and incredible understanding, and the breadth \add ≈of his interests seemed without limit\add*.
\v 30 \add *He\add* \add ≈was wiser than anyone in the countries east of Yisrael or in\add* Egypt.
\v 31 He was wiser than \add ≈anyone else\add*, including Eytan the Ezrahite and Mahol's son Heman and Kalkol and Darda, and his \add ≈reputation spread to\add* all the surrounding countries.\x + \xo 4:31: \xt Psa 89 header.\x*
\v 32 He \add ≈could tell\add* three thousand proverbs and he \add composed\add* over a thousand songs.\x + \xo 4:32: \xt Prv 1:1; 10:1; 25:1; Song 1:1.\x*
\v 33 He \add ≈could lecture on plant life\add* from the cedars in Lebanon to the hyssop that grows on walls. He \add ≈taught\add* about animals, birds, reptiles, and fish.
\v 34 People \add ≈from all around\add* came to listen to Shelomoh's wisdom—\add ≈many having been sent by other kings who'd heard about him\add*.
\c 5
\s1 The preparation of Shelomoh of making the temple
\rem /s1 Solomon Prepares to Build the Temple; Preparations for Building the Temple; Building of the temple; Preparations and Materials for the Temple
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 2:1-18)
\p
\v 1 Then King Hiram of Tsor \add !(Tyre)\add* sent his servants to Shelomoh¦158421 when he¦158422 heard that he'd been¦158422 anointed as king to¦158432 replace his father, because Hiram \add ≈had always been a friend of\add* David.
\v 2 Shelomoh¦158451 \add ≈replied\add* to Hiram,
\v 3 “You yourself knew David my father, \add ≡and¦158465 you knew\add* that he wasn't able to build a temple for his god Yahweh his god because of the \add ≈constant state\add* of warfare until Yahweh \add ≈would allow us to defeat all our enemies\add*.
\v 4 \add ≈But¦158488\add* now, my god Yahweh has \add ≈enabled us to¦158498 have peace¦158496\add* from¦158499 all¦158499 around¦158502. \add ≈We have no enemy, and¦158488 there's no threat of¦158501 danger\add*.
\v 5 \add ≈So\add* listen, I'm \add ≈planning\add* to¦158508 build a residence for my god Yahweh, just as Yahweh spoke to¦158515 my father David, ‘Your son who I'll put in your place on your throne, he'll \add be the one to\add* build the¦158520 house \add ≈to honour me\add*.’\x + \xo 5:5: \xt 2Sam 7:12-13; 1Ch 17:11-12.\x*
\v 6 \add ≈So\add* now, \add please give the\add* command to fell cedars for¦158531 me from the Lebanon \add forest\add*. Let my servants \add ≈accompany\add* your servants and¦158532 I'll \add ≈pay\add* your servants' wages \add ≈whatever amount you set\add*, because you yourself know that there are¦158525 no \add ≈other workers who know\add* how to fell trees like the Tsidonians.”
\p
\v 7 \add ≈When Hiram got Shelomoh's¦158545 message, he was¦158559 very happy\add* and¦158539 said, “Blessed be¦158559 Yahweh today, who has given David a wise son \add to rule\add* over that great \add ≈nation\add*.”
\v 8 \add *He\add* sent \add this message back\add* to¦158568 Shelomoh, “I've \add ≈received the¦158563 message\add* that you sent me and I myself will \add ≈supervise everything you want including¦158563\add* cedar and cypress \add ≈logs\add*.
\v 9 My servants will \add ≈transport them down from the forest\add* to the ocean¦158594, and I'll \add ≈get them tied into\add* rafts \add ≈and float them to the place you specify and disassemble the rafts\add*. \add ≈Then it'll be¦158591 up to you to get them where you need them\add*. \add ≈All I ask for, is¦158591 food for my family and workers\add*.”
\p
\v 10 \add ≈So\add* Hiram \add ≈supplied\add* all¦158601 the¦158598 cedar and¦158606 cypress \add ≈logs\add* that Shelomoh¦158599 \add ≈ordered\add*
\v 11 and¦158611 each¦158612 year Shelomoh \add ≈supplied\add* Hiram with¦158625 \add ≈three thousand tonnes\add* of wheat and \add ≈four hundred thousand litres of pure\add* \add olive\add* oil.
\v 12 Yahweh gave Shelomoh wisdom just like \add ≈he'd said¦158631 he¦158631 would\add*, and¦158635 there was¦158635 peace between Hiram and Shelomoh and the two of¦158632 them \add ≈made a treaty with each other\add*.
\p
\v 13 King Shelomoh \add ≈forced thirty thousand men from¦158645 all over Yisrael to¦158650 become his workers\add*,
\v 14 and¦158670 each¦158671 month he sent \add ≈a group of\add* ten thousand of them to¦158674 \add work in\add* Lebanon. \add @Each¦158670 group¦158670\add* \add ≈would spend\add* a month in Lebanon and then two months \add ≈at home\add* \add again\add*. Adoniram was \add ≈put in charge of all¦158671 that¦158683\add*.\x + \xo 5:14: \xt 1Ki 12:18.\x*
\v 15 Shelomoh¦158701 \add also\add* \add ≈forced eighty thousand men to¦158707 cut stones in¦158708 the¦158717 hill country and¦158691 seventy thousand men to haul loads\add*.
\v 16 Then¦158725 besides Shelomoh's¦158722 chief officers who were \add ≈in charge of\add* the work, there were \add another\add* three thousand three hundred supervisors of the workers.
\v 17 The¦158740 king commanded \add ≈them¦158735 to¦158735 quarry¦158735 large¦158735, valuable¦158735 blocks¦158735 of¦158735 dressed¦158735 stone¦158735 for¦158735 the¦158735 temple¦158735 foundations¦158735\add*.
\v 18 Shelomoh's and Hiram's workers and the Gebalites shaped the stones and prepared the timber for building the temple.
\c 6
\s1 Shelomoh begins temple construction
\rem /s1 Solomon Builds the Temple
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add So it was that\add* four¦159065 hundred¦159066 and¦159065 eighty¦159063 years¦159064 after¦159068 the Israelis¦159071 left¦159068 Egypt¦159074, in¦159063 the second¦159081 month¦159077 of the fourth¦159076 year¦159075 of Shelomoh's¦159083 reign¦159082 over¦159084 Yisrael¦159071, he¦159087 began the¦159088 construction of Yahweh's¦159089 residence.
\v 2 \add ≈It would be¦159097\add* twenty-seven¦159101 metres long¦159100, nine metres wide¦159102, and¦159091 fourteen metres high¦159105.\f + \fr 6:2 \ft About the height of a modern five-storey building (and the same height as Noah's barge).\f*
\v 3 \add ≈The¦159107 five-metre wide porch¦159107 on¦159122 the front¦159110 was¦159113 the same length¦159115 as the temple¦159111 width, so nine metres¦159122 long¦159115\add*.
\v 4 The window¦159131 \add openings\add* narrowed¦159133 \add towards the outer edge of the wall\add*.
\v 5 \add ≈Against¦159143 the outside of¦159138 the back wall and¦159135 the two sides\add*, he¦159150 built¦159135 \add three-storey (but lower than the wall)\add* side rooms¦159149.
\v 6 \add ≈The¦159154 lowest¦159156 level was¦159157 just over two metres¦159158 wide, the¦159160 middle¦159160 level was¦159161 half a metre wider, and¦159164 the top level the same again\add* \add (because the temple wall got thinner as it went up)\add*. \add ≈The temple walls had¦159175 ledges¦159169 built¦159170 into them for¦159171 the beams to sit on¦159158, so they wouldn't have¦159175 to go through¦159158\add* the walls¦159176.
\p
\v 7 While the temple¦159196 was being built¦159181, all of¦159189 the \add huge\add* stones¦159182 were dressed at the quarry¦159185 \add site\add*, so there was¦159186 no noise from any¦159189 hammer¦159187 or axe¦159188 or iron¦159192 tool \add ≈at the construction site\add*.
\p
\v 8 The¦159200 \add ≈entrance¦159199 to the bottom level of siderooms was¦159202 on¦159207 the south¦159206 side¦159204 of¦159204 the¦159205 temple¦159205, then from¦159212 there, stairs went¦159208 up¦159208 to¦159209 the¦159211 middle¦159211 level, and¦159212 more stairs to¦159215 the¦159217 top level\add*.
\v 9 \add ≈So¦159219\add* he¦159219 built¦159219 the¦159222 temple \add walls\add*, then¦159219 \add ≈it¦159223 was roofed¦159224 with¦159230 cedar¦159230 beams¦159228 and¦159219 planks¦159229\add*,
\v 10 and¦159232 he¦159232 built¦159232 the¦159235 \add ≈siderooms—each¦159239 level just over two metres¦159243 high¦159244 and¦159245 attached\add* to the¦159248 temple with¦159249 cedar¦159250 \add beams\add*.
\p
\v 11 Then Yahweh¦159256 \add ≈had this message¦159254 sent\add* to¦159257 Shelomoh¦159259,
\v 12 “This¦159263 residence that¦159264 you're¦159266 building¦159267, if¦159268 you¦159270 \add ≈comply with¦159271 my regulations¦159271 and¦159272 follow¦159275 my judgements¦159274 and¦159276 obey\add* all¦159279 my commands¦159281, then I'll¦159294 \add ≈ensure that everything I¦159284 promised¦159287 your¦159294 father¦159294 David¦159293 will¦159284 come¦159282 to¦159291 pass¦159282\add*.
\v 13 I will¦159296 live¦159296 among \add you\add* Israelis¦159299 and¦159296 I'll¦159301 never abandon¦159301 my¦159304 people¦159298 Yisrael¦159299.”
\s1 The temple furnishings
\rem /s1 The Temple's Interior; The Interior Furnishings of the Temple
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 3:8-14)
\p
\v 14 \add ≈So\add* Shelomoh¦159309 finished¦159313 the construction of the temple¦159312,
\v 15 and¦159315 on¦159321 the inside¦159320 he¦159315 made walls¦159318 of¦159318 cedar¦159322 boards. The¦159323 floor was made with¦159321 cypress¦159338 boards, and the walls were panelled from¦159331 the floor to¦159325 the ceiling¦159328.
\v 16 Inside¦159358 the temple¦159347, nine metres at¦159348 the¦159352 rear \add were closed off\add* to¦159353 make an¦159359 inner¦159359 room¦159359 called ‘the¦159360 very holy¦159360 place¦159361’. That too had walls¦159355 made of¦159360 cedar¦159349 boards¦159348.\x + \xo 6:16: \xt Exo 26:33-34.\x*
\v 17 That¦159367 left the¦159368 main temple¦159368 room as eighteen metres¦159364 long,
\v 18 and¦159371 it had cedar¦159371 boards on the¦159374 walls \add decorated with\add* carvings¦159376 of¦159376 buds and¦159378 flowers¦159379 \add ≈in blossom¦159379\add*. \add ≈All the¦159380 internal walls were¦159381 covered with cedar¦159381 so none of the large stones¦159383 were visible¦159384\add*.
\p
\v 19 The¦159387 inner¦159386 room¦159386 at the¦159389 back was to¦159392 house¦159389 the¦159396 box¦159396 containing Yahweh's¦159398 agreement¦159397,
\v 20 and¦159400 the¦159401 room¦159401 (nine metres long¦159404 and¦159405 wide¦159407 and¦159408 high¦159410) was¦159402 overlaid¦159411 with pure¦159413 gold¦159413 and¦159414 equipped with an¦159415 \add incense\add* altar¦159415 overlaid with cedar¦159416.
\v 21 Then¦159418 Shelomoh¦159419 overlaid¦159418 the¦159422 \add ≈main room\add* with pure¦159425 gold, and¦159418 he¦159426 \add hung\add* gold chains¦159427 crossing¦159426 across¦159426 the entrance to¦159430 the inner¦159431 room¦159431 that was overlaid¦159432 with gold¦159433.
\v 22 He¦159440 overlaid¦159440 \add the interior of\add* the entire¦159437 temple with gold¦159441 until¦159442 it was¦159444 all¦159445 finished¦159444, including¦159448 overlaying the¦159450 altar¦159450 belonging¦159453 to¦159453 the inner¦159453 room¦159453 with gold¦159455.\x + \xo 6:22: \xt Exo 30:1-3.\x*
\rem /s1 The Furnishings of the Temple
\p
\v 23 In¦159458 the inner¦159458 room¦159458, he¦159457 made two¦159459 winged creatures that were¦159464 five metres¦159465 high¦159466 and¦159457 made of¦159459 olive¦159463 wood¦159461.\x + \xo 6:23-28: \xt Exo 25:18-20.\x*
\v 24 Each wing¦159470 was¦159473 over two metres long, \add ≈giving¦159472 each¦159472 one¦159472 a total¦159472 wingspan¦159472\add* of¦159475 four and¦159468 a half metres.
\v 25 \add ≈Both creatures were¦159487 the¦159489 same size and¦159493 shape¦159493\add*,
\v 26 \add and\add* \add ≈they were¦159501 both five metres¦159502 high¦159498\add*.
\v 27 The¦159510 \add two\add* winged creatures \add ≈were¦159536 placed across the inner¦159514 room where their wingtips touched each other in¦159524 the centre and¦159507 their outer wings¦159518 touched the¦159523 walls¦159524\add*.
\v 28 \add Both of\add* the¦159545 \add ≈wooden carvings\add* were overlaid¦159542 with gold¦159546.
\p
\v 29 He¦159555 \add had\add* all¦159549 the¦159551 \add interior\add* walls¦159551 of¦159551 the¦159552 temple¦159552 engraved¦159556 with winged creatures and¦159559 palm¦159559 trees¦159559 and¦159560 flowers¦159561 \add ≈in bloom\add*,
\v 30 and¦159565 he¦159569 overlaid¦159569 the¦159567 floor¦159567 \add ≈of¦159567 both rooms\add* with gold¦159570.
\p
\v 31 He¦159577 made¦159577 doors¦159578 and¦159574 doorposts¦159583 for the¦159582 inner¦159576 room¦159576 with olive¦159581 wood¦159579,
\v 32 and¦159586 \add ≈both¦159586\add* doors¦159587 were \add ≈decorated\add* with carvings¦159593 of¦159593 winged creatures and¦159595 palm¦159595 trees¦159595 and¦159596 flowers¦159597 \add ≈in bloom\add*, then¦159586 they were overlaid¦159598 with gold¦159599 that was hammered¦159600 over the¦159603 carvings.
\v 33 He¦159612 made¦159612 the doorposts¦159615 for¦159613 the main temple¦159614 entrance¦159613 with¦159619 olive¦159618 wood¦159616,
\v 34 and¦159622 the¦159628 two folding doors¦159623 themselves each had two¦159626 folding sections made of¦159626 cypress¦159625.
\v 35 \add These doors were also decorated with\add* carvings of¦159640 winged creatures and palm¦159639 trees¦159639 and¦159640 flowers¦159641 \add ≈in bloom\add*, and then¦159637 overlaid¦159642 with gold¦159643 smoothed¦159644 down over the¦159647 carvings.
\p
